592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310433 13194139534791 true] [17592186157239 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310440 13194139534791 true] [17592186157785 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534791 true] [17592186157785 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186157785 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x - 6 = 3t, y - 3 = \\frac{3}{2}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139534791 false] [17592186157785 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186288353 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186288353 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 false] [17592186301496 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186301496 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 false] [17592186301503 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186301503 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 false] [17592186310391 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310392 13194139534791 true] [17592186310391 1089 "q-106461" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310391 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534791 true] [17592186310391 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310391 1113 #uuid "14e2d3b4-1c1a-54b9-9aa2-2d7f1398ec81" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310391 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310393 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310394 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310395 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310396 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310397 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1041 "selection" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310394 13194139534791 true] [17592186310392 1110 #uuid "1938660b-7d05-579b-9d0b-397ac95ff0de" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310393 1034 "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310393 1111 #uuid "1b885b18-3e83-562e-aa15-bede595287dc" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310394 1034 "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310394 1111 #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310395 1034 "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310395 1111 #uuid "eaf074c0-d547-57da-90fc-af45742a3986" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310396 1034 "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310396 1111 #uuid "5d878cd8-ea9d-571e-b2c4-2747cdeb4e68" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310397 1034 "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310397 1111 #uuid "5aebc749-f346-5867-826e-d05adad1e8d5" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310399 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1089 "q-84819" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1113 #uuid "1250f77b-d8f7-59d3-9273-1f7c3e0a3b34" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310398 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310400 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310401 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310402 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310403 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310404 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1041 "selection" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310402 13194139534791 true] [17592186310399 1110 #uuid "190ef792-c922-5d09-9b81-5dfb13cfb364" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310400 1034 "x-2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310400 1111 #uuid "e1e61765-b80b-504d-9903-e65e40eb0e8c" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310401 1034 "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310401 1111 #uuid "d80fe1bd-97bd-5de8-bcf7-9febea4261cd" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310402 1034 "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310402 1111 #uuid "859997d3-a529-5cab-a1c7-76920df6f3de" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310403 1034 "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310403 1111 #uuid "c5566a4d-7138-557e-b675-c0824617b854" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310404 1034 "x+2=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310404 1111 #uuid "912c272a-14e2-5247-bd7e-23ee7b3ae2e7" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310406 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1089 "q-84821" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1113 #uuid "14a70bce-c4a4-5acc-ac6d-0383921a9b0d" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310405 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310407 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310408 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310409 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310410 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310411 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1041 "selection" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310407 13194139534791 true] [17592186310406 1110 #uuid "545cf95e-06ea-56fe-a594-ff692f74aec8" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310407 1034 "x-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310407 1111 #uuid "2a097a16-8ddf-52c7-a590-ae96ec43f4e9" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310408 1034 "x+1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310408 1111 #uuid "5713448a-87f0-5942-a275-4f1cd9aaf1d4" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310409 1034 "x+1=-3t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310409 1111 #uuid "d3243e97-179a-552a-8f26-a1af3dedb265" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310410 1034 "x-1=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310410 1111 #uuid "9305d5cc-2022-5d4f-b678-b75458e98e03" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310411 1034 "x-1=-\\frac{4}{3}t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534791 true] [17592186310411 1111 #uuid "f716a89c-c284-50cc-9c82-ba1826cd4167" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310413 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1089 "q-84828" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1100 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,4)?$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1113 #uuid "09777cff-2521-5a71-be45-4262e5697b5e" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310412 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-1,4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=2pt \\\\ 1=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(1)t,\\,y-3=(1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=2t,\\,y-3={t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310414 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310415 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310416 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310417 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310418 13194139534791 true] [17592186310413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534791 tru